# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is git-ignored. Credentials are only ever read from the environment.
# Use sandbox or test accounts. Never put production credentials here for routine work.

# --- ATTIO ---
# Attio has no sandbox. Use a separate test workspace, and set ATTIO_TARGET to a label for it.
# With ATTIO_TARGET unset, every workspace is treated as production and --production is required.
# Workspace settings, Developers, New access token (a workspace admin creates it).
ATTIO_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# Label for the test workspace, for example sandbox. Not a secret.
ATTIO_TARGET=
# Scopes. Read-only pull, plan and drift: object_configuration:read, list_configuration:read
# Build (apply):  object_configuration:read-write, list_configuration:read-write
# Only to seed or assert records (not used by the adapter): add record_permission:read-write
# The workspace name and slug are read from GET /v2/self (no scope) and shown at confirmation.

# --- HUBSPOT ---
# Use a developer test account (Development > Testing > Test Accounts): it carries a 90-day Enterprise trial.
# Create a service key: Development > Keys > Service keys > Create service key (super admin).
# A legacy private app token also works. Either is sent as a Bearer token.
HUBSPOT_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# Label for the test account or sandbox, for example dev-test. Not a secret. The portal id is read from the API.
HUBSPOT_TARGET=
# Scopes. Read-only pull, plan and drift:
#   crm.schemas.contacts.read, crm.schemas.companies.read, crm.schemas.deals.read, crm.schemas.custom.read
#   crm.objects.contacts.read (any one crm.objects.*.read reads association labels and limits)
# Build (apply): the four crm.schemas.*.write scopes above, plus crm.objects.contacts.write,
#   crm.objects.companies.write, crm.objects.deals.write, crm.objects.custom.write (label and limit calls, OQ-14)
# Optional, not used by the adapter: crm.pipelines.governance.*, crm.pipelines.stage_permissions.*, crm.lists.*, automation
# The API version is fixed in tools/generators/hubspot.py (API_VERSION).

# --- SALESFORCE ---
# The adapter runs the Salesforce CLI (`sf`), which keeps its own logins. There is no secret in this file.
# 1. Install the CLI (@salesforce/cli) and sign in once as the build user, with a browser:
#      sf org login web --alias client-sbx --instance-url https://<MyDomain>--<SandboxName>.sandbox.my.salesforce.com
#    A production org uses its My Domain URL (ending my.salesforce.com). Use a sandbox or scratch org for routine work.
# 2. Put the alias (or the username) of that login here.
SF_TARGET_ORG=
# Optional. The API version for every call and for package.xml. Default 67.0 (the research pins it; see
# platforms/salesforce/reference/auth-and-setup.md). Raise it only when the org is on a newer release.
SF_API_VERSION=
# Optional. The CLI program name or full path. Default sf.
SF_CLI=
# The build user needs (auth-and-setup.md, "Permissions"):
#   Read-only pull, plan and drift:  API Enabled (describe and query). Business processes are read with a
#     retrieve, which needs the next row; without it the plan assumes the design's stages and says so.
#   Build (apply):  API Enabled, Modify Metadata Through Metadata API Functions and Customize Application
#     (or Modify All Data, which is more than a build needs). There is no read-only metadata permission.
#   Permission sets (the generated permission set is deployed by hand, not by a change): View Setup and
#     Configuration, and Manage Profiles and Permission Sets.
# Edition: the Metadata API needs Enterprise, Unlimited, Performance or Developer Edition. On Professional or
# Essentials the plan turns every change into a manual step and nothing is deployed.
# An org that is not a sandbox or scratch org is production: it needs --execute --production and typing the
# org's name. A Developer Edition org counts as production.
